          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01AEwb7j6KQDJHMznZ3CmK11",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022pages/concepts/retrieval-patterns.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Retrieval Patterns\\ndescription: Techniques for combining sparse and dense retrieval methods to optimize search accuracy across mixed query types.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Retrieval\\n  - concept/Ranking\\n  - concept/RAG\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022One authoritative source; concept validated through production RAG deployment patterns.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\n## Hybrid Retrieval\\n\\nHybrid retrieval combines sparse lexical search (e.g. BM25) with dense vector search, then merges the two ranked result sets, often via reciprocal rank fusion, to capture both exact term matches and semantic similarity.\\n\\nThis pattern consistently outperforms either method alone on queries mixing rare identifiers (error codes, product SKUs) with natural language intent, and has become the default retrieval pattern in production RAG systems.\\n\\n### Rationale\\n\\n- **Sparse retrieval** (BM25): Excels at matching exact terms and rare identifiers; can be brittle with synonyms or semantic variation\\n- **Dense retrieval** (vector search): Captures semantic similarity and paraphrasing; may miss exact matches or rare domain-specific terms\\n- **Hybrid fusion** (reciprocal rank fusion): Combines both signals to handle heterogeneous query patterns robustly\\n\\n### Reciprocal Rank Fusion\\n\\nReciprocal rank fusion is a score-agnostic method for merging ranked result sets from multiple retrievers. It reranks results by computing the reciprocal of their ranks in each result set, then sums those reciprocals. This approach is robust to differences in score scales across retrieval methods.\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
